{#
One shared entry, as it appears in a cell of the public month grid or a block of
its week and day grids.

The same object the app's own chip is: a coloured mark, the time, then whatever
label is left over — and the same two shapes for that mark, a bar where a block
has a height to fill and a dot where a month cell does not, because the shared
page's grids now go as narrow as the owner's do. It is NOT calendar/_event_chip.html.twig and cannot be —
that one is a <button> carrying an event id and an occurrence id, and it opens
an editor. There is no event behind this. A SharedOccurrence is a redaction with
no path back to the row it came from, which is the whole design (see the DTO),
so a chip drawn from one has nothing to open and is deliberately not focusable.

**The dot is the accent, and it is not the calendar's colour.** The app's chip
paints the calendar an event lives on; this one must not, and the rule is
ShareLinkReader's, stated where the merge is done: "a shared page says nothing
about the owner's calendars — not their names, not their colours, not how many
there are". Colours here would let somebody holding a busy/free link count the
diaries behind it and group a fortnight's blocks by which one they came from,
which is structure about the owner's life that no box on the form offered to
reveal. So every chip is one accent — the owner's accent, which is a fact about
their theme rather than about their calendars.

**No `title` attribute, ever.** A tooltip is the classic way a redacted page
starts leaking: it is invisible in review and it carries the full string.
SharedCalendarLeakTest reads the whole response body, so it would catch one —
but it is written here as well, because the next person adding a hover hint
should be told before the build tells them.

── Two shapes, one chip ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────

`tall` is the time-grid's chip and is deliberately a set of classes rather than
a second markup block, exactly as it is on the authenticated calendar's chip:
the same dot, the same time, the same label, filling a box that has a height of
its own instead of sitting on one line. Everything a chip IS stays one thing.

The two differ in one more thing than the app's chip does, and it is the reason
this note exists. Below `sm` the flat chip is the dot and nothing else: seven
columns of a month grid on a phone have room for a mark and not for a label, and
the agenda view is where the detail is read — which is the honest version of the
argument the old list-only page made, kept rather than thrown away. A tall chip
keeps its label at every width, because it is not in a 42-cell grid: it is in a
day column that is the width of the page, or in a week whose narrowness the
time-grid shell names as a known limitation and answers with the Day view.

Variables:
  entry       SharedOccurrence  already redacted; may carry nothing but times
  zone        string            the owner's zone, which the grid is drawn in
  timeFormat  string            a PHP date() format, from the install's clock
  tall        bool              optional, fill a positioned time-grid block
  showTime    bool              optional, print the start time (default true);
                                false on a band bar that began before the page
  until       string|null       optional, the end printed after the title on a
                                band bar for an entry of a day or more
#}
{% set tall = tall|default(false) %}
{# `??` rather than `|default`: the caller that passes this passes false. #}
{% set showTime = showTime ?? true %}
{% set until = until|default(null) %}

{# `chip-open` on the tall shape only, and only because the tall shape is the
   one that clips — see the utility in app.css. It is wanted here for exactly
   the reason it is wanted on the owner's grid: _time_grid_shell floors a column
   at 6rem and pans below that, so a shared week on a phone draws real blocks
   at a width where a title does not finish. The `hover:z-30` that lifts this
   over the day beside it is the shell's, on the positioned parent, because that
   parent declares the container and a container is a stacking context. #}
<div data-shared-entry
     class="group/chip flex gap-1.5 min-w-0
            {{ entry.isDetailed ? 'bg-accent/10' : 'bg-sunken/60' }}
            {{ tall
                ? 'h-full items-start overflow-hidden rounded-md ring-1 ring-line px-1.5 py-0.5
                   chip-open hover:shadow-lg'
                : 'items-center rounded-md px-0.5 sm:px-1.5 py-0.5' }}
            lit:ring-1 lit:ring-accent/50">
    {# Still one accent and never a calendar's colour — the rule above is
       unchanged by the shape. `self-stretch` rather than the `mt-1.5` this
       carried: a bar that fills the block needs no nudge to look placed. #}
    <span data-shared-entry-mark
          class="shrink-0 rounded-full {{ entry.isDetailed ? 'bg-accent' : 'bg-ink-faint' }}
                 {{ tall ? 'w-1 self-stretch' : 'w-1.5 h-1.5' }}"
          aria-hidden="true"></span>

    {% if not entry.isAllDay and showTime %}
        <span class="shrink-0 text-[11px] tabular-nums text-ink-faint
                     {{ tall ? 'leading-5' : 'hidden sm:inline' }}">
            {{ entry.startsAt|date(timeFormat, zone) }}
        </span>
    {% endif %}

    {# Truncated to one line in a cell, wrapped into the height it has in a
       block. `truncate` is `overflow:hidden` plus `white-space:nowrap`, so
       leaving it on a tall chip would waste every line but the first. #}
    <span class="min-w-0 flex-1 text-xs text-ink
                 {{ tall
                    ? 'whitespace-normal break-words line-clamp-3 leading-4 group-hover/chip:line-clamp-none'
                    : 'hidden sm:block truncate' }}">
        {{ entry.title ?? 'calendar.share.busy'|trans }}
    </span>

    {% if until is not null %}
        <span class="hidden sm:inline shrink-0 text-[11px] tabular-nums text-ink-faint whitespace-nowrap">{{ until }}</span>
    {% endif %}
</div>
